LIBTAM — THE TAMLINUX C FOUNDATION LIBRARY

libtam is the official C foundation library for Tamlinux packages, core system
daemons, hardware telemetry probes, and command-line utilities.


VISION & ANTI-BLOAT PHILOSOPHY

Tamlinux packages and utilities must be fast, rock-solid, and free of bloat.
Rather than pulling in heavyweight runtime environments or complex frameworks
like GLib, libtam provides a compact, cohesive standard C foundation designed
to:
- Enable writing complete system daemons and utilities in 150–300 lines of
  clean C.
- Maintain sub-megabyte resident memory (RSS) and instant 0–2 ms cold start.
- Natively compile against both glibc and musl on minimal Void Linux
  installations and legacy hardware.
- Enforce strict memory hygiene and timeout safety across all operations.


CAPABILITIES & MODULES

  Module: tam_str
  Header / Symbol: tam_str_*
  Description: Safe dynamic string manipulation with auto-growth, format
  appending (tam_str_cat_printf), trimming, and JSON string escaping
  (tam_str_cat_json).

  Module: tam_exec
  Header / Symbol: tam_exec, tam_exec_opts
  Description: Subprocess execution without a shell: a wall-clock deadline,
  standard input from /dev/null or supplied bytes, an output cap,
  stdout/stderr capture, and the whole process group killed on timeout.

  Module: tam_fs
  Header / Symbol: tam_fs_*
  Description: Safe filesystem primitives, whole-file reading, atomic writes
  that sync the file and its directory (tam_fs_write_atomic), directory sync,
  and mkdir -p (tam_fs_mkdir_p).

  Module: tam_fs_tree
  Header / Symbol: tam_fs_exchange, tam_fs_clone_or_copy, tam_fs_remove_tree,
  tam_fs_lock, tam_fs_walk
  Description: Whole-tree operations: swap two directories in one step, clone
  a file where the filesystem shares extents (else copy), remove a tree
  without following links or crossing filesystems, an exclusive lock with a
  timeout, and a sorted walk that never follows links.

  Module: tam_sha256
  Header / Symbol: tam_sha256_*
  Description: SHA-256 (FIPS 180-4): incremental, whole file, and hex.

  Module: tam_rec
  Header / Symbol: tam_rec_parse, tam_rec_cat
  Description: Line record files: one record per line, a leading format N
  line, a strict reader that refuses truncated or malformed files, and a
  writer that refuses fields it could not read back.

  Module: tam_path
  Header / Symbol: tam_path_*, tam_xdg_path
  Description: Path joining, joining under a root that refuses .. and absolute
  paths, a check for safe relative paths inside a tree (tam_path_is_safe_rel),
  and per-command XDG state, cache, data, config and runtime paths
  (<base>/tamlinux/<command>/).

  Module: tam_ini
  Header / Symbol: tam_ini_*
  Description: Lightweight INI and key-value file parser for user
  configuration and desktop state.

  Module: tam_uni
  Header / Symbol: tam_utf8_*, tam_uni_* in <tam_uni.h>
  Description: UTF-8 decode and encode, terminal display width from wcwidth,
  string width, and truncation to a cell count without splitting a sequence.

  Module: tam_key
  Header / Symbol: tam_key_* in <tam_key.h>
  Description: Byte-stream key decoder for ANSI/xterm terminals: UTF-8 text,
  control keys, CSI and SS3 sequences, modifiers, split sequences and an
  escape timeout.

  Module: tam_tty
  Header / Symbol: tam_tty_* in <tam_tty.h>
  Description: Raw mode with guaranteed restore, size and SIGWINCH support,
  alternate screen, cursor, SGR mouse, bracketed paste, and DEC 2026
  synchronized frames. ANSI/xterm only; no terminfo.

  Module: tam_term
  Header / Symbol: tam_log_*
  Description: Colorized terminal logging and terminal escape sequence
  utilities.

  Module: tam_portal
  Header / Symbol: tam_portal_* in <tam_portal.h>
  Description: Native desktop portal integration (FileChooser via D-Bus
  session bus). A separate library, libtam-portal.

Static libraries

An optional experimental third archive, libtam-display.a, now provides bounded
DRM/KMS inspection, owned restoration and atomic transactions. It has no
dependency on the core, portal, D-Bus or libdrm runtime. It uses installed
libdrm UAPI headers at build time. See the display API <docs/display.md> for
its ownership boundary and remaining feasibility gates. The optional
libtam-display-session.a adapter uses libsystemd for session observation and
seat validation, then existing permissions for direct device acquisition;
neither dependency enters the core or portal archives.

By default, libtam builds two static archives:

- libtam.a is the core. It needs only libc.
- libtam-portal.a holds the desktop portal. It needs D-Bus, so it is separate:
  a program that does not use the portal never links or loads D-Bus.

Programs link libtam statically, so each carries only the objects it uses (a
few kilobytes) and is tied to the exact libtam it was built and tested with.
Tamlinux packages rebuild every program from pinned sources when libtam
changes, so a shared library would save nothing there. Version 0.3.0 and
earlier also built a shared libtam.so; make install and make uninstall remove
it.


QUICKSTART

Building & Testing

    # Build libtam.a and libtam-portal.a
    make

    # Run the test suite under AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer
    make test

    # Optional display work: no portal or D-Bus development files needed
    make display test-display
    make install-display PREFIX=/your/development/prefix

    # Core-only development installation, also without portal dependencies
    make core install-core PREFIX=/your/development/prefix

    # Install to system
    sudo make install PREFIX=/usr/local

    # Or install a development build for your user, and remove it again
    make install PREFIX=$HOME/.local/dev
    make uninstall PREFIX=$HOME/.local/dev

make install also writes pkg-config files, libtam.pc and libtam-portal.pc, in
$(PREFIX)/lib/pkgconfig/. Programs that use libtam, such as tam, find it with
pkg-config libtam; programs that use the portal, such as tamlinux-tools'
tam-file-select, use pkg-config libtam-portal, which adds libtam and D-Bus.
For a prefix outside pkg-config's default search path, add it once in your
shell profile:

    export PKG_CONFIG_PATH="$HOME/.local/dev/lib/pkgconfig${PKG_CONFIG_PATH:+:$PKG_CONFIG_PATH}"

Usage Example

    #include <tam.h>
    #include <stdio.h>
    #include <stdlib.h>

    int main(void) {
        /* 1. Dynamic string formatting */
        tam_str_t s = tam_str_new("Tamlinux");
        s = tam_str_cat_printf(s, " v%s", tam_version());
        tam_log_info("Running: %s", s);

        /* 2. Subprocess execution with timeout (no shell) */
        const char *const cmd[] = {"uname", "-r", NULL};
        tam_exec_result_t res = tam_exec(cmd, 1000); /* 1000ms deadline */
        if (res.exit_code == 0) {
            tam_log_info("Kernel: %s", res.stdout_buf);
        }
        tam_exec_result_free(&res);

        /* 3. Atomic write to this command's state directory */
        char *path = tam_xdg_path(TAM_XDG_STATE, "example", "status", true);
        if (path != NULL) {
            tam_fs_write_atomic(path, s, tam_str_len(s), 0644);
            free(path);
        }

        tam_str_free(s);
        return 0;
    }


